Grids can also display right to left languages (like Arabic). The grid components check the dir attribute to determine content directionality. Using the same data as from the previous example, here is a grid rendered right to left. import { Grid } from "react-window";

function RtlExample({ contacts }: { contacts: Contact[] }) {
 return (
 <Grid
 cellComponent={CellComponent}
 cellProps={{ contacts }}
 columnCount={10}
 columnWidth={columnWidth}
 dir="rtl"
 rowCount={contacts.length}
 rowHeight={35}
 />
 );
}

Grid provides an imperative API for responding to events. The recommended way to access this API is to use the exported ref hook: import { useGridRef } from "react-window"; Attach the ref during render: function Example(props: Props) {
 const gridRef = useGridRef(null);

 return <Grid gridRef={gridRef} {...props} />;
} And call API methods in an event handler: const onClick = () => {
 const grid = gridRef.current;
 grid?.scrollToCell({
 behavior: "auto", // optional
 columnAlign: "auto", // optional
 columnIndex: 10,
 rowAlign: "auto", // optional
 rowIndex: 250
 });
}; The form below uses the imperative API to scroll the grid: Align Scroll behavior Job title Column Scroll The Grid API also provides scrollToColumn and scrollToRow methods for single-axis scrolling. Note If you are passing the ref to another component or hook, use the ref callback function instead. import { useGridCallbackRef } from "react-window";

function Example(props: Props) {
 const [grid, setGrid] = useGridCallbackRef(null);

 useCustomHook(grid);

 return <Grid gridRef={setGrid} {...props} />;
}

headings.text:
Common case In most cases, the useIsScrolling hook should be passed a reference to the list HTMLElement: import {
 List,
 useIsScrolling,
 useListCallbackRef,
 type RowComponentProps
} from "react-window";

function Example({ names }: { names: string[] }) {
 const [list, setList] = useListCallbackRef(null);
 const isScrolling = useIsScrolling(list?.element);

 return (
 <div className="h-full flex flex-col gap-2">
 <div className="text-xs text-slate-400">
 {isScrolling ? "Scrolling…" : "Not scrolling"}
 </div>
 <List
 listRef={setList}
 rowComponent={RowComponent}
 rowCount={names.length}
 rowHeight={25}
 rowProps={{ isScrolling, names }}
 />
 </div>
 );
}

function RowComponent({
 index,
 isScrolling,
 names,
 style
}: RowComponentProps<{ isScrolling: boolean; names: string[] }>) {
 return (
 <div className="flex items-center" style={style}>
 {isScrolling ? (
 <div className="h-3 w-24 rounded bg-white/10" />
 ) : (
 names[index]
 )}
 </div>
 );
}

Scrolling with the window For lists that scroll with the page , pass the string "window" instead: import { List, useIsScrolling } from "react-window";

function Example() {
 // The list itself doesn't scroll, so track the window instead
 const isScrolling = useIsScrolling("window");

 return (
 <List
 rowComponent={RowComponent}
 rowCount={1000}
 rowHeight={25}
 rowProps={{ isScrolling }}
 scrollElement="window"
 />
 );
}

Tables Rows in a <table> can't be absolutely positioned. Instead, rows can be rendered in normal document flow, with spacer rows taking the place of rows that aren't rendered. Using the <tbody> as the element (and the scrolling container as the scrollElement) positions rows relative to the body, so the sticky header doesn't offset them: import { useState } from "react";
import { useVirtualizer } from "react-window";

function Example({ addresses }: { addresses: Address[] }) {
 const [scrollElement, setScrollElement] = useState<HTMLDivElement | null>(
 null
 );
 const [tbody, setTbody] = useState<HTMLTableSectionElement | null>(null);

 // Rows are positioned relative to the <tbody>, so the header doesn't offset them
 const { items, totalSize } = useVirtualizer({
 count: addresses.length,
 element: tbody,
 itemSize: 25,
 scrollElement
 });

 // Spacer rows take the place of rows that aren't rendered
 const first = items[0];
 const last = items[items.length - 1];
 const spaceBefore = first ? first.offset : 0;
 const spaceAfter = last ? totalSize - last.offset - last.size : 0;

 return (
 <div
 className="h-55 overflow-auto"
 ref={setScrollElement}
 // Rows scrolled to (e.g. with scrollToIndex) are aligned below the sticky header
 style={{ scrollPaddingTop: 25 }}
 >
 {/* Fixed layout keeps column widths from changing as different rows are rendered */}
 <table
 aria-rowcount={addresses.length + 1}
 className="w-full table-fixed"
 >
 <colgroup>
 <col className="w-1/2" />
 <col className="w-1/3" />
 <col />
 </colgroup>
 <thead className="sticky top-0 bg-teal-600">
 <tr aria-rowindex={1} className="h-[25px]">
 <th className="px-2 text-left">City</th>
 <th className="px-2 text-left">State</th>
 <th className="px-2 text-left">Zip</th>
 </tr>
 </thead>
 <tbody ref={setTbody}>
 {spaceBefore > 0 && (
 <tr aria-hidden style={{ height: spaceBefore }} />
 )}
 {items.map(({ index, size }) => {
 const address = addresses[index];

 // Add 2 to the index to account for the header row
 return (
 <tr
 aria-rowindex={index + 2}
 key={index}
 style={{ height: size }}
 >
 <td className="px-2 truncate">{address.city}</td>
 <td className="px-2 truncate">{address.state}</td>
 <td className="px-2 text-xs">{address.zip}</td>
 </tr>
 );
 })}
 {spaceAfter > 0 && <tr aria-hidden style={{ height: spaceAfter }} />}
 </tbody>
 </table>
 </div>
 );
} Like the browser's scrollIntoView, scrollToIndex aligns items within the scroll-padding of the element being scrolled. Setting scroll-padding-top to the height of the sticky header keeps rows from being aligned underneath it. The aria-rowcount and aria-rowindex attributes tell assistive technologies how many rows the table has, even though only some of them are rendered.

Dynamic sizes Items with dynamic row heights must be passed to the measureElement ref callback and specify a data-index attribute: import { useState } from "react";
import { useDynamicRowHeight, useVirtualizer } from "react-window";

function Example({ lorem }: { lorem: string[] }) {
 const [element, setElement] = useState<HTMLDivElement | null>(null);
 const itemSize = useDynamicRowHeight({ defaultRowHeight: 50 });

 const { items, measureElement, totalSize } = useVirtualizer({
 count: lorem.length,
 element,
 itemSize
 });

 return (
 <div className="h-full overflow-auto" ref={setElement}>
 <div className="relative" style={{ height: totalSize }}>
 {items.map(({ index, offset }) => (
 <div
 className={cn("absolute w-full p-2", {
 "bg-white/10": index % 2 === 0
 })}
 // Rows are measured after rendering, so they need their index
 data-index={index}
 key={index}
 ref={measureElement}
 style={{ transform: `translateY(${offset}px)` }}
 >
 {index}: {lorem[index]}
 </div>
 ))}
 </div>
 </div>
 );
}

text:
The List and Grid components render their own markup. To render custom markup instead (e.g. a <table>), use the useVirtualizer hook. It calculates which items to render and where; how they are rendered is up to you. import { useState } from "react";
import { useVirtualizer } from "react-window";

function Example({ names }: { names: string[] }) {
 const [element, setElement] = useState<HTMLDivElement | null>(null);

 const { items, totalSize } = useVirtualizer({
 count: names.length,
 element,
 itemSize: 25
 });

 return (
 <div className="h-full overflow-auto" ref={setElement}>
 <div className="relative" style={{ height: totalSize }}>
 {items.map(({ index, offset, size }) => (
 <div
 className="absolute w-full"
 key={index}
 style={{ height: size, transform: `translateY(${offset}px)` }}
 >
 {names[index]}
 </div>
 ))}
 </div>
 </div>
 );
} The hook returns the items to render (each with an index, offset, and size), the totalSize of all items, the visibleRange, and a scrollToIndex method. Its options mirror the props of List: count, itemSize, overscanCount, direction, scrollElement, and onItemsRendered.

Libraries like this help to render a lot of items as efficiently as possible by limiting how many items are rendered at once. Here is an over-simplified illustration of a list with 6 columns. Only 3 or 4 columns are rendered at a time because that is enough to fill the viewport. (The user can't see the other columns, so we don't need to render them). 1 2 3 4 5 6 When a user scrolls the list, a different set of columns are rendered- but always only a few at a time. The illustration above shows unrendered columns as dimmed. In reality, they aren't there at all. The columns that do get rendered are positioned using CSS properties like left to mimic other columns before them. To render one of these items, all you need to provide is a component like the one below. import { type RowComponentProps } from "react-window";

/**
 * Example row.
 *
 * @param index Specifies which row you're rendering
 * @param style CSS properties like "position" and "top"
 */
function Row({ index, style }: RowComponentProps) {
 return <div style={style}>Row {index}</div>;
}

Lists that load their data as the user scrolls can use the onRowsRendered callback to decide when to load more. For example, a feed can load the next page when the user scrolls near the end: import { useState } from "react";
import { List, type RowComponentProps } from "react-window";

const PAGE_SIZE = 50;
const TOTAL_PAGES = 10;

// Start loading the next page when the user scrolls within this many rows of the end
const THRESHOLD = 10;

function Example() {
 const [items, setItems] = useState<string[]>([]);
 const [isLoading, setIsLoading] = useState(false);

 const hasMore = items.length < PAGE_SIZE * TOTAL_PAGES;

 // fetchPage loads a page of items (e.g. from a server)
 const loadNextPage = async () => {
 setIsLoading(true);
 try {
 const page = await fetchPage(items.length / PAGE_SIZE, PAGE_SIZE);
 setItems((prev) => [...prev, ...page]);
 } finally {
 // Reset even if loading fails, so the page can be requested again
 setIsLoading(false);
 }
 };

 return (
 <List
 onRowsRendered={({ stopIndex }) => {
 // Only load one page at a time
 if (hasMore && !isLoading && stopIndex >= items.length - THRESHOLD) {
 loadNextPage();
 }
 }}
 rowComponent={RowComponent}
 // Render an extra row to show that more items are loading
 rowCount={hasMore ? items.length + 1 : items.length}
 rowHeight={25}
 rowProps={{ items }}
 />
 );
}

function RowComponent({
 index,
 items,
 style
}: RowComponentProps<{ items: string[] }>) {
 return (
 <div className="flex items-center px-2" style={style}>
 {index < items.length ? (
 items[index]
 ) : (
 <span className="text-slate-500">Loading…</span>
 )}
 </div>
 );
} Loading state prevents the same page from being requested more than once while the user scrolls.

The ARIA list role can be used to identify a list of items. <div role="list">
 <div
 role="listitem"
 aria-posinset="1"
 aria-setsize="1000"
 >
 Row 1
 </div>

 <div
 role="listitem"
 aria-posinset="2"
 aria-setsize="1000"
 >
 Row 2
 </div>

 <!-- More rows ... -->
</div> The List component automatically adds this role to the root HTMLDivElement it renders, but because individual rows are rendered by your code- you must assign ARIA attributes to those elements. To simplify this, the recommended ARIA attributes are passed to the rowComponent in the form of the ariaAttributes prop. The easiest way to use them is just to pass them through like so: import { type RowComponentProps } from "react-window";

function RowComponent({
 ariaAttributes,
 names,
 index,
 style
}: RowComponentProps<{
 names: string[];
}>) {
 return (
 <div style={style} {...ariaAttributes}>
 {names[index]}
 </div>
 );
}

Sometimes the height of a row isn't known until it's been rendered. Here is an example list of lorem ipsum text of varying sizes. Each row can also be toggled collapsed/expanded by clicking on the "+"/"-" button. For this kind of list, react-window provides a helper hook called useDynamicRowHeight. import { useMemo, useState } from "react";
import { List, useDynamicRowHeight } from "react-window";
import { RowComponent } from "./ListRowDynamicRowHeights";

function Example({ lorem }: { lorem: string[] }) {
 const listState = useListState(lorem);

 const rowHeight = useDynamicRowHeight({
 defaultRowHeight: 50
 });

 return (
 <List
 rowComponent={RowComponent}
 rowCount={lorem.length}
 rowHeight={rowHeight}
 rowProps={{ listState }}
 />
 );
} In this case, rows can just render their content as they normally would and react-window will measure it for you. import { type RowComponentProps } from "react-window";

function RowComponent({
 index,
 listState,
 style
}: RowComponentProps<{
 listState: ListState;
}>) {
 const isCollapsed = listState.isRowCollapsed(index);
 const text = listState.getText(index);

 return (
 <div
 className={cn("p-2 cursor-pointer", {
 "bg-white/10": index % 2 === 0,
 truncate: isCollapsed
 })}
 onClick={() => listState.toggleRow(index)}
 style={style}
 >
 <ToggleIcon isCollapsed={isCollapsed} /> {index}: {text}
 </div>
 );
} Dynamic row heights are not as efficient as predetermined sizes. It's recommended to provide your own height values if they can be determined ahead of time. Dynamic-height lists scroll to the target row instantly, even when smooth scrolling is requested.

The simplest type of list to render is one with fixed row heights. To render this type of list, you need to specify how many rows it contains (rowCount), which component should render rows (rowComponent), and the height of each row (rowHeight): import { List } from "react-window";

function Example({ names }: { names: string[] }) {
 return (
 <List
 rowComponent={RowComponent}
 rowCount={names.length}
 rowHeight={25}
 rowProps={{ names }}
 />
 );
} The rowProps object can also be used to share data between components. Values passed in rowProps will also be passed as props to the row component: import { type RowComponentProps } from "react-window";

function RowComponent({
 index,
 names,
 style
}: RowComponentProps<{
 names: string[];
}>) {
 return (
 <div className="flex items-center justify-between" style={style}>
 {names[index]}
 <div className="text-slate-500 text-xs">{`${index + 1} of ${names.length}`}</div>
 </div>
 );
} Lists require vertical space to render rows. Typically the ResizeObserver API is used to determine how much space there is available within the parent DOM element. If an explicit height is specified (in pixels) using the style prop, ResizeObserver will not be used.

Images are an example of dynamic row heights. As with text, the useDynamicRowHeight hook can be used to measure images. import { List, useDynamicRowHeight } from "react-window";

function Example({ images }: { images: string[] }) {
 const rowHeight = useDynamicRowHeight({
 defaultRowHeight: 250
 });

 return (
 <List<RowProps>
 rowComponent={RowComponent}
 rowCount={images.length}
 rowHeight={rowHeight}
 rowProps={{ images }}
 />
 );
} A loading placeholder might be rendered until the image has been measured for the first time. import { type RowComponentProps } from "react-window";

type RowProps = {
 images: string[];
};

function RowComponent({ index, images, style }: RowComponentProps<RowProps>) {
 const url = images[index];

 return (
 <div style={style}>
 <LoadingSpinner className="absolute z-[-1]" />
 <img className="w-full" src={url} />
 </div>
 );
}

headings.text:
scrollToRow Scrolls the list so that the specified row is visible.
Like the browser's scrollIntoView, items are aligned within the CSS scroll-padding of the element being scrolled
(e.g. scroll-padding-top can leave room for a sticky header).
Dynamic row heights are measured after rendering;
Content that resizes later may require another scrollToRow call.
 behavior "smooth" is ignored for dynamic height lists;
scrollToRow is instant as it requires rows to be lazily measured

 align Determines the vertical alignment of the element within the list
 behavior Determines whether scrolling is instant or animates smoothly
 index Index of the row to scroll to (0-based)

 scrollToRow: ({
 align,
 behavior,
 index
 }: {
 align?: "auto" | "center" | "end" | "smart" | "start";
 behavior?: "auto" | "instant" | "smooth";
 index: number;
 }) => void;

Hooks The useListRef hook returns a mutable ref object. function Example(props: Props) {
 const listRef = useListRef(null);

 return <List listRef={listRef} {...props} />;
} And the useListCallbackRef hook returns a ref callback function. This is better when sharing the ref with another hook or component. import { useListCallbackRef } from "react-window";

function Example(props: Props) {
 const [list, setList] = useListCallbackRef(null);

 useCustomHook(list);

 return <List listRef={setList} {...props} />;
}

text:
By default, lists and grids scroll their own content. To scroll a list along with the rest of the page instead (e.g. a feed), pass "window" as the scrollElement prop: import { List } from "react-window";

function Example({ names }: { names: string[] }) {
 return (
 <List
 rowComponent={RowComponent}
 rowCount={names.length}
 rowHeight={25}
 rowProps={{ names }}
 scrollElement="window"
 />
 );
} The list renders at its full height and only renders the rows that are visible within the viewport. Its position on the page is measured whenever it renders, scrolls, or resizes, and whenever content before it is resized, inserted, or removed (e.g. an image loading above it). A list can also scroll along with other content inside of a scrollable element. Pass the element as the scrollElement prop (it will be null until it has mounted): import { useState } from "react";
import { List } from "react-window";

function Example({ names }: { names: string[] }) {
 const [scrollElement, setScrollElement] = useState<HTMLDivElement | null>(
 null
 );

 return (
 <div className="h-full overflow-auto" ref={setScrollElement}>
 <div className="p-2 mb-2 rounded bg-slate-700">
 Content above the list
 </div>
 <List
 rowComponent={RowComponent}
 rowCount={names.length}
 rowHeight={25}
 rowProps={{ names }}
 scrollElement={scrollElement}
 />
 <div className="p-2 mt-2 rounded bg-slate-700">
 Content below the list
 </div>
 </div>
 );
} The imperative API scrolls the scroll element, and sticky rows stick to the top of the scroll element. Grids support this prop too. Rows scroll with the scroll element; columns also scroll with it if the grid is as wide as its content. Otherwise the grid fills the width of its parent and its columns scroll within it. import { Grid } from "react-window";

function Example({ contacts }: { contacts: Contact[] }) {
 return (
 <Grid
 cellComponent={CellComponent}
 cellProps={{ contacts }}
 columnCount={10}
 columnWidth={columnWidth}
 rowCount={contacts.length}
 rowHeight={25}
 scrollElement="window"
 // Columns scroll within the grid unless it is as wide as its content
 style={{ width: "max-content" }}
 />
 );
} When a scroll element is provided: defaultHeight (and defaultWidth for grids) are used as the viewport size until the scroll element has been measured (e.g. during server rendering) Percentage row heights (and column widths) are relative to the scroll element's viewport Default scroll offsets are ignored; the list doesn't control the scroll element's position

List provides an imperative API for responding to events. The recommended way to access this API is to use the exported ref hook: import { useListRef } from "react-window"; Attach the ref during render: function Example(props: Props) {
 const listRef = useListRef(null);

 return <List listRef={listRef} {...props} />;
} And call API methods in an event handler: const onClick = () => {
 const list = listRef.current;
 list?.scrollToRow({
 align: "auto", // optional
 behavior: "auto", // optional
 index: 250
 });
}; The form below uses the imperative API to scroll the list: Align Scroll behavior State Scroll Note If you are passing the ref to another component or hook, use the ref callback function instead. import { useListCallbackRef } from "react-window";

function Example(props: Props) {
 const [list, setList] = useListCallbackRef(null);

 useCustomHook(list);

 return <List listRef={setList} {...props} />;
}

If you want to render content on top of your list or grid, the safest method is to use a portal and render them directly into the parent document. This avoids potential clipping issues or z-index conflicts. For the specific case of "sticky" rows, you can render within the parent list or grid using the children prop: The example above was created using code like this: import { List, type RowComponentProps } from "react-window";

function Example() {
 return (
 <List
 rowComponent={RowComponent}
 rowCount={101}
 rowHeight={20}
 rowProps={EMPTY_OBJECT}
 >
 <div className="w-full h-0 top-0 sticky">
 <div className="h-[20px] bg-teal-600 px-2 rounded">Sticky header</div>
 </div>
 </List>
 );
} Note the height of 0 in the example above prevents the sticky row from affecting the height of the parent list.

Many types of tabular data can be rendered using the list component. The example above uses Flexbox layout to position columns and headers. import { getScrollbarSize, List, type RowComponentProps } from "react-window";

function Example({ addresses }: { addresses: Address[] }) {
 const [size] = useState(getScrollbarSize);

 return (
 <div className="h-55 flex flex-col">
 <div className="flex flex-row bg-teal-600 p-1 px-2">
 <div className="grow flex flex-row items-center gap-2 font-bold">
 <div className="flex-1">City</div>
 <div className="flex-1">State</div>
 <div className="w-10">Zip</div>
 </div>
 <div className="shrink" style={{ width: size }} />
 </div>
 <div className="overflow-hidden">
 <List
 rowComponent={RowComponent}
 rowCount={addresses.length}
 rowHeight={25}
 rowProps={{ addresses }}
 />
 </div>
 </div>
 );
}

function RowComponent({
 index,
 addresses,
 style
}: RowComponentProps<{
 addresses: Address[];
}>) {
 const address = addresses[index];

 return (
 <div className="flex flex-row items-center gap-2 px-2" style={style}>
 <div className="flex-1">{address.city}</div>
 <div className="flex-1">{address.state}</div>
 <div className="w-10 text-xs">{address.zip}</div>
 </div>
 );
} It may be more efficient to render data with many columns using the Grid component.

Lists with rows of different types may require different heights to render. Here is an example of the most populous US postal codes, grouped by state. State "header" rows are taller and are styled differently. This list requires a rowHeight function that tells it what height a row should be based on the type of data it contains. type Item =
 | { type: "state"; state: string }
 | { type: "zip"; city: string; zip: string };

type RowProps = {
 items: Item[];
};

function Example({ items }: { items: Item[] }) {
 return (
 <List<RowProps>
 rowComponent={RowComponent}
 rowCount={items.length}
 rowHeight={rowHeight}
 rowProps={{ items }}
 />
 );
}

function rowHeight(index: number, { items }: RowProps) {
 switch (items[index].type) {
 case "state": {
 return 30;
 }
 case "zip": {
 return 25;
 }
 }
}

headings.text:
Sizing Lists and grids normally measure their available space using a ResizeObserver, but there is nothing to measure on the server. Instead, use the defaultHeight prop (and defaultWidth prop for grids) to tell react-window how much space to fill. This determines how many rows (and columns) are server rendered. import { List } from "react-window";

function Example({ names }: { names: string[] }) {
 return (
 <List
 className="h-100"
 defaultHeight={400}
 rowComponent={RowComponent}
 rowCount={names.length}
 rowHeight={25}
 rowProps={{ names }}
 />
 );
} Alternatively, specify an explicit pixel size using the style prop (e.g. style={{ height: 400 }}). This size is used on both the server and the client, so no measurement is needed at all. Without a default size, only the first row (and any overscan rows) will be server rendered. Once the page hydrates, the list will measure itself and render the remaining rows. Percentage-based sizes (e.g. rowHeight="25%") can't be resolved without a size. Unless a default size (or a pixel size via the style prop) is specified, they are server rendered with a size of 0 and rows will overlap until the page hydrates. The default size only determines how many rows are rendered before the list has been measured; the list's actual size is still determined by CSS. To server render more rows than are visible (e.g. for crawlers that don't run JavaScript), set defaultHeight to a value larger than the list's height. The extra rows will be removed once the page hydrates, but keep in mind that hydration cost grows with the number of rows rendered.

text:
Rows with dynamic heights can't be measured until they are rendered in a browser. Until then, server-rendered rows are displayed in normal document flow (rather than being positioned using estimated heights) so that they don't overlap or leave gaps. Once the page hydrates and rows have been measured, they are positioned as usual. To reduce layout shift once rows have been measured, per-row heights can be provided to useDynamicRowHeight using the initialRowHeights option. These heights are used in place of the default height until rows are measured. For example, they might be estimated from each row's content: import { useState } from "react";
import { useDynamicRowHeight } from "react-window";

const LINE_HEIGHT = 20;
const PADDING = 10;

function useRowHeight(messages: string[]) {
 // Only read when the hook first mounts, so there's no need to recompute it
 const [initialRowHeights] = useState(() =>
 messages.map(
 (message) => message.split("\n").length * LINE_HEIGHT + PADDING
 )
 );

 return useDynamicRowHeight({
 defaultRowHeight: 50,
 initialRowHeights
 });
} Heights can also be measured ahead of time, e.g. by rendering the list in a headless browser while generating a static site. When rendering from a Server Component, compute the heights on the server and pass them as a prop to the client component that calls useDynamicRowHeight. If row heights can be determined exactly ahead of time, use a rowHeight function instead; it's more efficient than measuring rows. Initial heights are matched to rows by index, and dynamic heights often depend on the list's width (e.g. text that wraps). Heights that were computed for different data or a different width will cause layout shift once rows are measured. Changing the hook's key option re-reads initialRowHeights.

Use the defaultScrollOffset prop (or the defaultScrollLeft and defaultScrollTop props for grids) to set the initial scroll position. import { List } from "react-window";

const ROW_HEIGHT = 25;

function Example({
 initialIndex,
 names
}: {
 initialIndex: number;
 names: string[];
}) {
 return (
 <List
 className="h-100"
 defaultHeight={400}
 defaultScrollOffset={initialIndex * ROW_HEIGHT}
 rowComponent={RowComponent}
 rowCount={names.length}
 rowHeight={ROW_HEIGHT}
 rowProps={{ names }}
 />
 );
} HTML can't be pre-scrolled, so the server always renders the first rows of the list. The default scroll offset is applied once the page hydrates (or, for lists that are initially empty, once they have rows), unless the list has already been scrolled (e.g. by the browser restoring a previous scroll position).

List and Grid are client components, but they can be rendered directly by a Server Component as long as all of their props can be sent from the server to the client: // app/names/page.tsx (a Server Component)
import { List } from "react-window";
import { RowComponent } from "./RowComponent";

export default async function Page() {
 const names = await loadNames();

 return (
 <List
 className="h-100"
 defaultHeight={400}
 rowComponent={RowComponent}
 rowCount={names.length}
 rowHeight={25}
 rowProps={{ names }}
 />
 );
} The rowComponent (or cellComponent) must be a client component, marked with the "use client" directive. Values in rowProps (or cellProps) must be serializable. // app/names/RowComponent.tsx
"use client";

import { type RowComponentProps } from "react-window";

export function RowComponent({
 index,
 names,
 style
}: RowComponentProps<{ names: string[] }>) {
 return <div style={style}>{names[index]}</div>;
} Functions can't be passed from the server to the client, so props like rowHeight functions, rowKey, onRowsRendered, onResize, and listRef (as well as hooks like useDynamicRowHeight) need to be used within a client component: // app/names/NamesList.tsx
"use client";

import { useCallback } from "react";
import { List, useDynamicRowHeight } from "react-window";
import { RowComponent } from "./RowComponent";

export function NamesList({ names }: { names: string[] }) {
 const rowHeight = useDynamicRowHeight({
 defaultRowHeight: 25
 });

 const rowKey = useCallback((index: number) => names[index], [names]);

 return (
 <List
 className="h-100"
 defaultHeight={400}
 rowComponent={RowComponent}
 rowCount={names.length}
 rowHeight={rowHeight}
 rowKey={rowKey}
 rowProps={{ names }}
 />
 );
}
